<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Where to look, and who is there.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The page you leave a skyBlip Go on: where to look, who is there, and what the alarm means when it speaks.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What you see in flight</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="heading-up">Traffic drawn <span class="highlight">the way you see it</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the middle, track up. The picture stays that way as the sky
        turns around you. Traffic drawn to the upper right is traffic to look
        for off the right of the nose, with nothing to convert. Nothing
        is written across the top of the screen, because that is the half you are
        flying into.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each aircraft is a shape that tells you where to look.
        A point facing up means above you. A point facing down means below. A diamond means at your
        level. A small angled mark shows if it is climbing or descending. The line
        from each symbol ends where the aircraft will be in a minute.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft drawn as diamonds, one above, one below and one level and climbing, each with a line showing where it will be in a minute, your own aircraft's projected track dashed with a dot at each of the next two minutes, the four-mile ring, the flight time and the count of aircraft heard" %>
      <figcaption>Radar</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Track up, never north up</li>
    <li>Ring of 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM</li>
    <li>The shape says above, level or below</li>
    <li>A minute of track ahead of each aircraft</li>
    <li>The count of every aircraft heard</li>
    <li>Flight time, for the logbook</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="every-aircraft">Every aircraft, <span class="highlight">however far</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        To scale, the picture ends at the edge of the screen: with the 4&nbsp;NM ring, an
        aircraft beyond about six miles disappears. With
        <span class="mono">PLOT</span> on <span class="mono">ALL</span>, the
        first option in the settings menu and the default on a new device,
        every aircraft heard with a position is shown. The ring
        shrinks to leave a band around it. An aircraft beyond the ring appears
        on that band, in its direction, with the same shape it would have inside.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its distance is not shown to scale, but its minute of track is: from the aircraft's
        real position, with only the part inside the ring drawn. An aircraft
        closing fast from six miles appears as a mark on the band and a line already
        inside the ring, ending where it will be in a minute.
      </p>
      <p>
        Aircraft close together on the band share one mark with a count beside
        it. An aircraft triggering an alarm takes priority. Otherwise, the mark shows the aircraft
        closest to your altitude. The count never covers another aircraft, and
        a mark behind you moves aside rather than disappear
        under the range label.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "far-all.png", alt: "The radar page with PLOT ALL: a smaller ring with two aircraft inside it, and on the band around it an aircraft ahead with its projected track entering the ring, a mark with the count 3 for a group to the upper right, and one aircraft far to the left" %>
      <figcaption>PLOT ALL</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>ALL, the default: every aircraft with a position</li>
    <li>TO SCALE: the picture drawn to the ring's scale</li>
    <li>On the band: the direction, the shape, the alarm</li>
    <li>A minute of track, where it enters the ring</li>
    <li>Aircraft close together on the band share one mark and a count</li>
    <li>The corner shows the count of every aircraft heard</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    <%= link_to_page "displays", label: "The screens page", anchor: "both-ways" %>
    shows the same traffic in both modes, one button press apart.
  </p>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="alarm">One alarm, and it says <span class="highlight">where to look</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        An aircraft within 3&nbsp;km horizontally and 300&nbsp;m vertically has its symbol filled
        in. A quarter of the screen centred on it goes black once a second. You
        have nothing to read: you catch the flash out of
        the corner of your eye. It tells you to turn your
        head that way. Whatever page you are on, the radar takes over.
      </p>
      <p>
        The black sector catches the eye. The symbol gives you the angle.
        Traffic 38° off the nose is not rounded to &ldquo;ahead and
        right&rdquo;: the sector is centred on 38°, so you look there rather
        than search a quarter of the sky.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no higher alert level, and there never will be. Climbing or
        descending has to be coordinated with the other aircraft. This
        device has no link for that. The loudest thing it can say is look.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during a traffic advisory: a black 90-degree sector centred on the direction of the traffic, with the filled diamond of the converging aircraft and its one-minute projected track shown in white against it" %>
      <figcaption>Traffic advisory</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>3&nbsp;km around you, 300&nbsp;m above and below</li>
    <li>A quarter of the screen, centred on the traffic</li>
    <li>One flash a second</li>
    <li>The buzzer, vibration and LED alert you too</li>
    <li>One touch of the pad silences it</li>
    <li>A caution, never an instruction</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="shapes">Six shapes, and <span class="highlight">you read them without learning them</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    A point facing up means traffic above you. A point facing down means below. A diamond means
    traffic within 60&nbsp;m of your altitude. A full-size symbol means the aircraft is within the 300&nbsp;m
    altitude range used by the alarm. A smaller symbol means it is further away vertically. It
    stays on the picture without ever triggering the alarm. The angled mark appears from
    2.5&nbsp;m/s of climb or descent, pointing in the direction the traffic is going.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The ring means an aircraft is sending no altitude. It points neither up nor down, because
    nobody knows if it is above or below you. Within 3&nbsp;km it is filled
    like any alarm, because it may be at your level.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    A symbol filled in black means an alarm. While no symbol is filled, nothing is
    urgent: it is the one contrast the screen keeps in reserve.
  </p>

  <%= render "shared/blips",
        plain: "Plain",
        alarm: "Alarm",
        climbing: "climbing",
        steady: "steady",
        sinking: "sinking",
        above: "Point up",
        above_when: "above you, to 300 m",
        level: "Diamond",
